{{also|forgó}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|forego||proscribed}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|forgon|t=to go by, pass up}}, from {{inh|en|ang|forgān|t=to go away, forgo}}; equivalent to {{pre|en|for|go}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fɔːˈɡəʊ/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-forgo.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/fɔɹˈɡoʊ/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|oʊ|s=2|q1=UK|q2=General American}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|forgoes|forgoing|forwent|forgone}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# To [[do without]] (something enjoyable); to [[relinquish]].
#: {{ux|en|I've had to '''forgo''' my student discount in exchange for taking the job.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=February|title=Talking of Trains|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=67|text=Mr. Hoyle, who does not believe many multiple-unit diesel services on secondary routes will resist for ever the road transport challenge, would '''forgo''' passenger traffic altogether on a little-used route in order to improve the quality of the freight working and reduce its costs by equating the average speed of all trains on the line concerned.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1986|journal=New York Magazine|volume=19|issue=49|page=20
|passage=You might think that Americans buy roughly the same number of fitted sheets as flats. Or, considering the market for electric blankets, duvets, and other covers, that consumers buy even more bottom sheets, simply '''forgoing''' the tops.}}
# {{lb|en|literary}} To [[refrain]] from, to [[abstain]] from.
#: {{ux|en|I shall '''forgo''' actually stepping inside, thank you.}}

====Usage notes====
Not to be confused with {{m|en|forego||go before}}, though {{m|en|forego||do without}} is also sometimes used as an alternative spelling of '''forgo'''.

====Quotations====
* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|III|iii|page=13|passage={{...}}Doe not for one repulſe '''forgoe''' the purpoſe / That you resolu'd t' effect.}}

